  { q: 'Can ravens point like humans?', a: 'Yes — a 2011 study by Pika and Bugnyar documented ravens using bill-pointing gestures (holding up objects in the bill while orienting toward a partner) and gaze-following in ways functionally similar to pointing in human infants. These gestures appeared to serve communicative functions — drawing a partner\'s attention to objects — rather than purely individual manipulation. Referential gesturing in birds was previously thought to be absent or limited; the raven study was among the first documented cases in a non-primate species.' },
